    file(path        = lang/java.ixml
         size        = 27486
         sha256      = bb2f96d6f9bf47b3894bb170bd3dc78e4bce9cbbbba56585304d02d0960cdae7
         mtime       = 2026-11-20T20:49:16Z
         version     = 0g
         description = '''A Java 17 grammar written in IXML by Fredrik Öhrström 2026.

                          Copyright (c) 2026 Fredrik Öhrström libxmq (spdx: MIT)

                          Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy
                          of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
                          in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights
                          to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell
                          copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is
                          furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:

                          The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all
                          copies or substantial portions of the Software.

                          THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
                          IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,
                          FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE
                          AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER
                          LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,
                          OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE
                          SOFTWARE.

                          The grammar parses Java 17 source files, which are UTF-8. The tree has the
                          contents of the code: names, literals, operators, comments and the structure.
                          Whitespace and punctuation are dropped.

                          The grammar is written for the LL(*) parser generator (xmqj generate-parser)
                          as well as the ixml engine, in the style of library/lang/c.ixml:

                            - Whitespace and comments (ws) are placed at the end of each token, except
                              in the first rule, which accepts leading white space. No rule may start
                              with ws, otherwise whitespace can be split ambiguously. A comment on the
                              line of the token is a comment, a comment on the following lines is a
                              leading_comment, which java.xslq moves to the declaration or statement
                              that follows it.
                            - Tokens use the not operator ! to implement maximal munch, e.g. an
                              IDENTIFIER cannot be followed by another identifier character (!idchar) and
                              the token + cannot be followed by + or =. Keywords cannot be identifiers
                              (!keyword). The contextual keywords (var, yield, record, sealed, permits)
                              are identifiers, matched as keywords only where the syntax needs them.
                            - > is always a single token, so that List<List<String>> closes two type
                              argument lists. The shift operators >> and >>> are > tokens without
                              whitespace between them. The assignments >>= and >>>= are tokens.
                            - Unicode escapes (A) are only recognized inside char, string and
                              text block literals.
                            - Literals keep their source text without the quotes, escapes are not
                              translated, e.g. "a\n" is STRING_LITERAL = a\n

                          The tree, eg for public static <T> List<T>[] f(final int x) throws E { ... }

                            method(name = f) {
                                modifier = public
                                modifier = static
                                type_parameters { type_parameter(name = T) }
                                type { name = List  type_arguments { type { name = T } }  dim }
                                parameter(name = x) { modifier = final  type { primitive = int } }
                                throws { type { name = E } }
                                block
                            }

                            - Declared names are attributes: class(name = A), variable(name = x), ...
                            - A type has a primitive (int) or a dotted name (java.util.List), then its
                              type_arguments, and a dim for every []. A name is split only where type
                              arguments or annotations come between its parts:
                              Outer<String>.Inner is type { name = Outer  type_arguments {...}  name = Inner }
                            - wildcard { extends {...} } or wildcard { super {...} } for ? extends T.
                            - Annotations and modifiers are kept in source order.
                            - void, varargs, static (of an initializer) and dim are empty elements.
                            - A comment keeps its text without // or /* */, eg comment = ' the x'. After
                              java.xslq the comments on their own lines before a declaration or
                              statement are its first children, eg the javadoc of a method, the
                              comments before a closing brace are the last children of the block, and
                              a comment on the line of a token is after the element of the token.

                          The grammar accepts more than Java in a few places: an expression statement
                          can be any primary (a; is accepted, Java requires a call, x++ or an
                          assignment) and the left side of an assignment can be any primary (1 = x;).

                          Not supported: annotation type declarations (@interface), annotations on
                          types (eg List<@NonNull String>, annotations before a type are modifiers),
                          method references on a generic type (List<String>::size),
                          module-info.java and the Java 21 features (record patterns, patterns in switch).''')
    file(path        = lang/java.xslq
         size        = 22426
         sha256      = 8b5433331fcf38c100d888bd6d2c70b97881ffb7ab4bd2c251d31107364dfc55
         mtime       = 2026-11-20T20:49:16Z
         version     = 0g
         description = 'Cleanup after the ixml parse of Java with java.ixml, version v0g as the grammar.
                        Fredrik Öhrström (c) 2026 spdx: MIT

                        The grammar is written for the generated LL(*) parser, so its expressions have an
                        element for every precedence level they pass and the method calls, field and array
                        accesses are chains of selectors on a primary. This stylesheet:

                          - removes the levels without an operator, eg assignment { conditional_expression {
                            logical_or { postfix { name = x } } } } is name = x, and names a level with an
                            operator after the operator, eg additive for + and -,
                          - conditional_expression with ? : is conditional and a level with instanceof is
                            instanceof,
                          - a primary (or postfix) with a selector is method_call, field_access,
                            array_access, method_reference, new (outer.new), this (Outer.this), super
                            (Outer.super) or class_literal (a.b.class),
                          - a chain of names is a dotted name, a.b.c is name = a.b.c, and a method call on
                            it has the dotted name: a.b.c(x) is method_call(name = a.b.c) { arguments }.
                          - a leading comment (on its own line) is moved to the declaration or statement
                            that follows it, or to the end of the block before a closing brace, and the
                            comments of a token with text, eg a name, are after its element.

                          xmq ixml:lang/java Foo.java           (uses this cleanup automatically)
                          xmq library/lang/java.ixml Foo.java transform library/lang/java.xslq')
