                        The grammar is written for the generated LL(*) parser, so its expressions have an
                        element for every precedence level they pass and the method calls, field and array
                        accesses are chains of selectors on a primary. This stylesheet:

                          - removes the levels without an operator, eg assignment { conditional_expression {
                            logical_or { postfix { name = x } } } } is name = x, and names a level with an
                            operator after the operator, eg additive for + and -,
                          - conditional_expression with ? : is conditional and a level with instanceof is
                            instanceof,
                          - a primary (or postfix) with a selector is method_call, field_access,
                            array_access, method_reference, new (outer.new), this (Outer.this), super
                            (Outer.super) or class_literal (a.b.class),
                          - a chain of names is a dotted name, a.b.c is name = a.b.c, and a method call on
                            it has the dotted name: a.b.c(x) is method_call(name = a.b.c) { arguments }.
                          - a leading comment (on its own line) is moved to the declaration or statement
                            that follows it, or to the end of the block before a closing brace, and the
                            comments of a token with text, eg a name, are after its element.

                          xmq ixml:lang/java Foo.java           (uses this cleanup automatically)
                          xmq library/lang/java.ixml Foo.java transform library/lang/java.xslq')
    file(path        = measurement/metar-to-html.xslq
         size        = 96112
         sha256      = 8ed4a83ddae881839af5e3335e23fa192f46f5b9235cf642257ffc5ff152e0f8
         mtime       = 2026-11-20T22:15:06Z
         version     = 1b
         description = 'Render a METAR report, parsed with metar.ixml, as a web page:
                          xmq ixml:measurement/metar metar.inp xsl:measurement/metar-to-html to-html > metar.html

                        A blue header shows the station, the time, the flight category (VFR, MVFR,
                        IFR, LIFR) and the temperature, dew point, pressure and visibility.

                        The wind is drawn on a compass with a bezel and a compass rose, the arrow flies
                        with the wind from the direction it blows from, the variation is drawn as a
                        wedge. The hub shows the speed, colored by its strength, and the Beaufort force
                        is printed below. Every weather group gets an icon on a sky tile with cloud,
                        sun, rain, snow, ice, lightning and fog.

                        The cloud layers are drawn as a cross section of the sky at their altitudes,
                        with as many clouds as the layer has oktas, cumulonimbus and towering cumulus
                        as towers, the precipitation falling from the lowest layer and the vertical
                        visibility as a hatched fog. Every layer is labeled with its okta symbol.
                        Everything is also printed in words in the table below.

                        The groups after a trend (BECMG, TEMPO) are the forecast, they are not drawn
                        but printed with the trend.')
